@@ -0,0 +1,53 @@
+---
+id: task-003-rfc-6902-conformance-of-applypatch-validation-and-errors
+type: task
+title: "RFC 6902 conformance of applyPatch: validation and errors"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Task — RFC 6902 conformance of applyPatch: validation and errors
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` report surprising results: some patches they consider invalid are accepted, some edge
+cases give unexpected results, and a patch that fails does not always behave as RFC 6902 reads. Review how
+patches are checked and applied, and make the behaviour conform to RFC 6902 in every case, errors included.
+
+## Findings (probed on the approved task-002 implementation)
+
+- An invalid operation after a failing one is reported as the operation failure (`Error`, "test failed"), not
+  as an invalid patch (`SyntaxError`). Operations are validated one at a time, interleaved with application.
+- A value that is not a JSON value is accepted: `undefined`, `NaN`, `Infinity`, `Date`, `Map`, class instances,
+  sparse arrays and cyclic structures. `undefined` is stored as a member, and a function fails inside
+  `structuredClone` with a `DOMException`.
+- `op`, `path`, `from` and `value` are read through the prototype chain, so an inherited member counts as a
+  member of the operation. RFC 6902 operations are JSON objects, whose members are their own.
+
+## Scope
+
+- The whole patch is checked before any operation is applied: a malformed patch throws `SyntaxError`, whatever
+  its operations would do. Only a valid patch whose operation fails throws `Error`.
+- A `value` of `add`, `replace` and `test` must be a JSON value (RFC 8259): null, boolean, finite number, string,
+  array or plain object, recursively, with no holes and no cycles.
+- Operation members are read as own properties.
+- Patch array holes are rejected as invalid operations.
+- Documentation (doc comment and README) states the checks and the error types.
+- No change to the approved semantics of the six operations, which already match RFC 6902 section 4.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. A patch that is not an array, or whose operation is not a valid operation, throws `SyntaxError` before any
+   operation is applied, even when an earlier operation would fail.
+2. `add`, `replace` and `test` reject a `value` that is not a JSON value with `SyntaxError`: `undefined`, `NaN`,
+   infinite numbers, functions, symbols, bigints, non-plain objects, sparse arrays, cyclic structures, and any of
+   these nested in a valid container.
+3. Inherited members do not count: an operation or value read from the prototype chain is an invalid operation.
+4. Valid JSON values of every kind are accepted, including `null`, `false`, `0`, `""` and nested containers.
+5. The approved behaviour is unchanged for valid patches; the existing tests still pass.
+6. The input document and patch are not mutated; no partial result is returned.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- Validating the input document: it is assumed to be JSON, as before.
+- JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396) and other extensions.
